---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="My plan, and what is not in it — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="This post tells where I want LightPhotos to go. Your photos stay on your drive. Any AI runs on your own computer. Only the basic features must work on every platform."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-20">September 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>My plan, and what is not in it</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          This post tells where I want LightPhotos to go. Your photos stay on
          your drive. Any AI runs on your own computer. Only the basic features
          must work on every platform.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          This post tells where I want LightPhotos to go in the next year. Your
          photos stay as files on your own drive, and any AI features will run
          on your own computer. Only the basic features must work on every
          platform. I also want keyboard access to all features, with shortcuts
          that you can change to the keys that you already know. I plan to make
          the full app open source. The plan has no dates, and its main job
          is to give me a list that I can use to say no to other features.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 256" role="img" aria-label="Three rows. Each row pairs a part of the plan with an item that I will not do. Photos stay on your drive, not on a photo platform of my own. AI runs on your computer, and no AI uploads your photos. Basic features work on every platform, but not every feature works on all four.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">THE CORE OF THE PLAN</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="40">I WILL DO THIS</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="336" y="40">I WILL NOT DO THIS</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="52" width="304" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="76">Photos stay on your drive</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="95">Files in your folders. No account.</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="336" y="52" width="304" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="352" y="76">My own photo platform</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="95">No upload, no library that I keep</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" stroke-width="2" d="M610 74 L622 86 M622 74 L610 86"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="120" width="304" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="144">AI runs on your computer</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="163">An idea to try: ComfyUI</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="336" y="120" width="304" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="352" y="144">AI that uploads your photos</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="163">To servers of a different company</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" stroke-width="2" d="M610 142 L622 154 M622 142 L610 154"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="188" width="304" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="212">Basic features everywhere</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="231">Mac, Windows, Linux, the browser</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="336" y="188" width="304" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="352" y="212">Every feature on all four</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="231">That ships fewer, plainer features</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" stroke-width="2" d="M610 210 L622 222 M622 210 L610 222"></path>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Each part of the plan has a matching item that I will not do. For
          me, the right column is the most useful part.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write down what you will not build.</strong> If you decide
          on features one at a time, a small tool slowly becomes a large tool.
          A written list kept the first version small. I will use the same
          method for the next year.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not every feature must run on every platform.</strong> If
          every feature must work on all four platforms, I will ship fewer
          features. I would rather have a feature on the Mac than not have it at all.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Know the cost of the tools of a platform before you use them.</strong>
          Apple frameworks do a lot of work for you, but you cannot take that
          work with you to other platforms. I learned this when I ported my code away from Image I/O.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If I ask for your trust, you should be able to check.</strong>
          I say that your photos never leave your drive. If the full app is open
          source, you can check that yourself.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        I wrote many posts about how LightPhotos works, but no post about its
        direction. I decided its direction one feature at a time. That is how a
        small tool slowly becomes a large tool.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is the plan. For me, the most useful part is what I am leaving
        out. It is the same idea as the seven-item list in
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">the first post</a>.
        But this list is for the next year, not for the first afternoon.
      </p>

      <h2>Your photos stay yours</h2>
      <p>
        I will not build a service that stores your photos. You do not create
        an account, you do not upload photos, and I do not keep a library for
        you. Your photos are files on your drive. LightPhotos opens the folder
        that already contains them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I did not start with this principle. I learned it from a bad
        experience. I lost years of photos because only one app could read
        their library format. I wrote about that in the first post. That
        experience still controls most of these decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also want LightPhotos to access drives that are not connected to your
        computer. The first one will be Google Drive. The problem is speed. If
        a photo tool stops each time that you push the arrow key for the next
        photo, it is not good enough to ship. Thus I will do this only if the
        performance is good enough, and I do not know yet if it will be.
      </p>

      <h2>I am not cloning Lightroom</h2>
      <p>
        Lightroom has a lot of really good features, and I will not try to match
        them. The AI features are the strongest part. Almost all of them send
        your photo to servers that a different person or company controls. I
        said that I will never do that.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, I want to look at ComfyUI. ComfyUI is a tool that runs AI
        image models on your own computer. I did not build any of this yet.
        So it is an idea that I want to try, not a promise. I like that the
        model runs on the same computer as your photos, and nothing leaves your
        drive.
      </p>

      <h2>Not every feature on every platform</h2>
      <p>
        If every feature must work the same on all four platforms, I will ship
        fewer features. Also, the features that I ship will be the plainest
        versions.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I put features into two groups, and I use different rules for
        each group. The basic features that all users expect already run on all
        platforms: the browser, Windows, Linux, and the Mac. With these
        features, you can browse a folder, rate and filter photos, make basic
        adjustments, and export. Similar features that I build next will also
        go into this group.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 216" role="img" aria-label="Two panels. The left panel, Basic features, shows macOS, Windows, Linux and the browser. It lists browse, rate, filter, adjustments and export, from one Rust codebase that compiles to WASM. The right panel, Mac only, lists Core Image, Vision and VisionKit, Image I/O, AVFoundation and Object Capture. These are Apple frameworks that are difficult to port.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHAT RUNS WHERE</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="380" height="180" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="48">Basic features</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="66">One Rust codebase, compiled to WASM for the browser</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="80" width="78" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="55" y="97" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="106" y="80" width="78" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="145" y="97" text-anchor="middle">Windows</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="196" y="80" width="78" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="235" y="97" text-anchor="middle">Linux</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="286" y="80" width="78" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="325" y="97" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="130">Open any folder, no import step</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="148">Rate and filter</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="166">Exposure, highlights, shadows, white balance</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="184">Export to JPEG</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="400" y="24" width="240" height="180" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="416" y="48">Mac only</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="416" y="66">Apple frameworks, difficult to port</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="416" y="88" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="432" y="96">Core Image</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="416" y="110" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="432" y="118">Vision and VisionKit</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="416" y="132" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="432" y="140">Image I/O</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="416" y="154" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="432" y="162">AVFoundation</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="416" y="176" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="432" y="184">Object Capture</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The left side is already built and runs on all four platforms. The
          right side is an experiment. Features on the right side can stay
          Mac-only for a long time.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Beyond these basic features, I want to find out how much Apple
        frameworks can do for me. Apple supplies these code libraries for
        Mac apps. The ones I mean are Core Image, Vision and VisionKit, Image
        I/O, AVFoundation, and Object Capture. Object Capture makes 3D models
        from photos. I already know the result from
        <a href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">porting away from
        Image I/O</a>. Apple frameworks do a lot of work for you for free, but
        you cannot take any of it with you to other platforms.
      </p>
      <p>
        This time, I expect that result before I start. Features that use
        those frameworks will probably be Mac-only, possibly permanently. I would
        rather have a feature on one platform than not have it at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting your photos out</h2>
      <p>
        I do not want LightPhotos to be your photo library. So it must be
        easy to send your photos to a photo library. I plan to support Immich,
        a photo library that you can host yourself. I also plan to support
        export to cloud storage in general.
      </p>
      <p>
        For me, the important thing is that export works the same way for a
        server on your home network and a server on the internet. I do not
        want two different features, with one of them only half complete.
      </p>

      <h2>Everything from the keyboard</h2>
      <p>
        I want keyboard access to every feature. Then you can use the full app
        without the mouse. This applies to all features, not only the common
        features.
      </p>
      <p>
        One reason is that I wanted keyboard shortcuts from a photo tool from
        the start. Shortcuts are also why Luminar did not fit how I work. The other reason is that when I
        <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">chose to build everything in Rust</a>, I
        gave up the accessibility that browsers give you for free. I said this at
        that time. Keyboard support is the part of that problem that I can
        correct myself, so I make it a requirement.
      </p>

      <h2>Shortcuts you can change</h2>
      <p>
        What I hate most about photo apps is that each app uses a different set
        of keys. Luminar AI, RawTherapee and Darktable each want you to learn
        their own keys. That is a large part of the reason that I do not want
        to use them.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you changed photo apps before, you probably know that feeling. You
        already know how you like to work, but the app tells you to start
        again. I do not want LightPhotos to do that to anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I want you to be able to change every keyboard shortcut in
        LightPhotos. If you already know a set of keys from a different app,
        you can use those keys here. You do not have to learn my keys.
      </p>

      <h2>Open source, all of it</h2>
      <p>
        I plan to make the full codebase open source. I do not mean a core
        library without the interesting parts. I also do not mean an old
        version that I release later. I mean the full app.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reason is the same as for the storage decision. I ask you to trust
        that your photos stay on your drive and that nothing goes to a server.
        You should be able to check that yourself.
      </p>

      <h2>The next app works the same way</h2>
      <p>
        This plan is not only for one photo editor. I want to build other Rust
        apps in the same way. I now follow that pattern on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, I build a native Mac app. I use a Mac, and I want the first
        version to be for a user that I know well. Then I use WebAssembly to
        move the basic features to the browser. After that, I move them to the
        other platforms that people use.
      </p>
      <p>
        I worked out that order with LightPhotos. Three methods came from this
        codebase. The first is to share the basic features across all
        platforms, but let the other features be different. The second is to
        keep the core editing code in a module that is separate from the
        interface. The third is to weigh the cost of the frameworks of a
        platform before I use them. I expect to use those methods again without
        changes.
      </p>

      <h2>The plan on one page</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Not doing</th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">Doing</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Storage</th>
              <td>A photo platform of my own</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Local drives, and Google Drive if it is fast enough</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">AI</th>
              <td>Anything that uploads your photos</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Investigating ComfyUI on your own computer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Platforms</th>
              <td>Every feature on all four</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Basic features on all platforms, Apple frameworks on the Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Sharing</th>
              <td>Becoming your library</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Immich, and export to local or remote servers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Input</th>
              <td>Mouse-first design</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Every feature reachable from the keyboard</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Shortcuts</th>
              <td>A fixed set of keys that you must learn</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Shortcuts that you can change to match how you work</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Code</th>
              <td>A binary that you must trust</td>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Open source, the full app</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>What the list is actually for</h2>
      <p>
        This plan has no dates. I work on LightPhotos at irregular times, so
        the deadlines in a quarterly roadmap would only be guesses.
      </p>
      <p>
        What the list really does is give me a reason to say no to other
        features. The first version shipped because I wrote down seven things
        and left everything else out. This list uses the same idea, but
        it is larger.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also want people to argue with it. If something here is wrong, or
        if an obvious item is missing, tell me in
        <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">the issue
        tracker</a>. I will read it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current builds are on the <a href="/downloads.html">downloads
        page</a>. To try LightPhotos without an installation, use the browser
        version at <a href="/app.html">the web app</a>.
      </p>

